[{"id":3652081938,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOAO6SJc7ZrlkS","user":{"login":"aaronfranke","id":1646875,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2NDY4NzU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1646875?u=24618295fece29a0bf5c850faf2555985232f5f2&v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/aaronfranke","html_url":"https://github.com/aaronfranke","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aaronfranke/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aaronfranke/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aaronfranke/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aaronfranke/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aaronfranke/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aaronfranke/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aaronfranke/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aaronfranke/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aaronfranke/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"I am generally in favor of switching this, since this means the parser/importer will have the same behavior as the writer/exporter, and since this avoids precision loss for integers larger than 2^53. The behavior would be generally more useful for the cases I can think of.\r\n\r\nHowever, this has major implications for all users of the JSON class, both user code and engine code. For user code, there's not much we can do about it, we'd just need user projects to convert numbers if needed, and handle both int/float if checking the type of a number that is read from JSON. As for engine code, it is likely that there will be many problems only discovered after merging, so this PR would need to be merged very early in a release cycle, such as before the first dev release of 4.7.\r\n\r\nThe code makes sense to me from a short review. `String::to_int` will indeed return INT64_MAX/INT64_MIN when beyond the range, and 